            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for to give rise to, then the Portuguese for thanks to."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: o impacto",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o impacto — \"the impact\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A nova fábrica teve um grande impacto na aldeia. The new factory had a big impact on the village."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ter impacto em — to have an impact on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "impacto is a learned borrowing from Latin impāctus (EtymDB), the participle of impingere \"to push, to strike or drive into\" (Lewis & Short), built from in and pangere \"to fix\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
